Feature: AWS Security Lake ingest of AI activity

  A security team already collects CloudTrail into AWS Security Lake as OCSF Parquet files. An
  organisation admin adds the lake as a governance source: LangWatch subscribes as a Security
  Lake data access subscriber, reads each new Parquet file the subscriber queue announces, keeps
  the AI activity (Bedrock model and agent calls, AgentCore, SageMaker endpoint invocations) and
  stores it in the governance OCSF events next to the events pushed in and pulled from providers.
  Agents that never send traces to LangWatch show up through the calls they make.
  Docs: docs/ai-gateway/governance/ocsf-export.mdx

  Background:
    Given an Enterprise organisation with an internal governance project
    And a Security Lake source reading CloudTrail management and data events

  Rule: an admin configures the source with the subscriber the lake created

    @unit
    Scenario: A Security Lake source reads CloudTrail management and data events by default
      Given an admin saves a Security Lake source with a Region, subscriber role ARN and queue URL
      When the configuration is read
      Then it reads the CLOUD_TRAIL_MGMT, S3_DATA and LAMBDA_EXECUTION sources

    @unit
    Scenario: A Security Lake source with a malformed role ARN or Region is refused by field
      Given an admin saves a Security Lake source with the role ARN "not-an-arn" and the Region "Europe"
      When the configuration is checked
      Then the complaints name "Subscriber role ARN" and "Region"

    @unit
    Scenario: Test connection reads the queue backlog without taking a message
      Given a Security Lake source whose subscriber queue holds two messages
      When the admin tests the connection
      Then the test reports the queue reachable with two messages waiting
      And both messages are still on the queue

    @unit
    Scenario: Test connection reports the AWS error when the queue refuses
      Given a Security Lake source whose queue refuses the stored access keys
      When the admin tests the connection
      Then the test reports the queue unreachable with the AWS error

    @unit
    Scenario: Test connection assumes the subscriber role with the external id
      Given a Security Lake source with a subscriber role that trusts LangWatch with the external id
      When the admin tests the connection
      Then LangWatch sends AssumeRole with the source's generated external id before it reads the queue

    @unit
    Scenario: Test connection checks the settings in the form before they are saved
      Given a saved Security Lake source
      When the admin changes the queue URL and the access keys and tests the connection
      Then the test reads the changed queue with the changed keys
      And nothing is saved

    @unit
    Scenario: LangWatch generates the external id of a Security Lake source
      Given an admin adds a Security Lake source and sends an external id of their own
      When the source is saved
      Then the source gets a random external id generated by LangWatch
      And the external id sent is ignored
      And the external id is stored sealed with the access keys, never in the plain settings
      And an edit with new access keys keeps the external id

    @unit
    Scenario: The reader refuses to assume the role without the generated external id
      Given a Security Lake source on AWS whose sealed credentials hold no external id
      When the scheduled pull runs
      Then the run fails before any AWS request

    @unit
    Scenario: A source without its own key pair never uses the worker's AWS identity
      Given the worker runs with AWS credentials of its own
      When a Security Lake source with no keys, blank keys, or no keys and a non-AWS endpoint is read
      Then the read is refused before any AWS request is signed
      And a source with its own keys signs every request with those keys only

    @unit
    Scenario: Only an organisation admin can add or edit a Security Lake source
      Given a member without the ingestion sources manage permission
      When the member saves a Security Lake source
      Then the request is refused as forbidden before the handler runs

    @unit
    Scenario: Adding or editing a Security Lake source needs the Enterprise plan
      Given an organisation without the Enterprise plan
      When an admin adds or edits a Security Lake source in the settings
      Then the request is refused before the source is saved

    @unit
    Scenario: The generic source create refuses the Security Lake adapter under another source type
      Given an organisation with or without the Enterprise plan
      When an admin creates an OpenTelemetry source through the generic sources route with the adapter aws_security_lake and a pull schedule
      Then the create is refused as an unsupported source type
      And no source is stored

    @unit
    Scenario: The generic source edit refuses to give a source the Security Lake adapter
      Given a push source with no stored pull adapter
      When an admin edits it through the generic sources route to the adapter aws_security_lake
      Then the edit is refused as an unsupported source type
      And the source is not changed

    @unit
    Scenario: The scheduled pull refuses the Security Lake reader for a source of another type
      Given a stored source of type otel_generic whose pull adapter is aws_security_lake
      When its scheduled pull runs
      Then the run is refused with a message pointing at the Security Lake page
      And the Security Lake reader never runs

    @integration
    Scenario: A Security Lake source name or description past its limit is refused
      When an admin adds or edits a source with a name over 128 characters or a description over 2,000 characters
      Then the request is refused by field and nothing is stored
      And a request body over 1 MiB is refused before it is read

    @integration
    Scenario: A service key adds a Security Lake source with no user as its creator
      Given an organisation service key with the ingestion sources manage permission
      When the key adds a Security Lake source over the REST API
      Then the source is saved with no user as its creator

    @integration
    Scenario: A Security Lake source with an empty or non-cron schedule is refused
      When an admin adds a Security Lake source with an empty schedule or one that is not a five-field cron expression
      Then the request is refused as invalid and nothing is stored

  Rule: the reader keeps AI activity and writes it once per OCSF event

    @unit
    Scenario: Bedrock calls in a CloudTrail Parquet file are stored and the other events dropped
      Given the queue announces a Parquet file with a Bedrock InvokeModel call, a Converse call and an S3 GetObject call
      When the source is read
      Then the two Bedrock calls are stored with source type "security_lake"
      And each stored event names the caller principal ARN and the model id
      And the S3 GetObject call is dropped

    @unit
    Scenario: A redelivered message does not duplicate events
      Given a Parquet file that was read and written
      When the queue delivers its notification again
      Then the stored events keep the same identities and their count is unchanged

    @unit
    Scenario: An event without a metadata uid gets a stable identity
      Given a Bedrock call with no metadata uid
      When the same file is read twice
      Then both reads write it under the same identity

    @unit
    Scenario: A message is deleted only after its events are written
      Given the governance store refuses the write
      When the source is read
      Then the read fails and the message stays on the queue

    @unit
    Scenario: A file outside the selected sources is skipped and its message deleted
      Given the source reads only CLOUD_TRAIL_MGMT
      And the queue announces a file under the S3_DATA source
      When the source is read
      Then nothing is stored and the message is deleted

    @unit
    Scenario: The S3 test event and SNS-wrapped notifications are understood
      Given the queue holds the S3 test event and an SNS-wrapped S3 notification
      When the messages are parsed
      Then the test event is ignored and the wrapped notification names its bucket and key

    @unit
    Scenario: An EventBridge Object Deleted notification is acknowledged without a read
      Given the queue holds an EventBridge "Object Deleted" notification for a selected source
      When the source is read
      Then no object is fetched and the message is deleted without poison

    @unit
    Scenario: A slow read keeps its messages hidden from other readers
      Given a batch of messages whose objects take longer to read than the queue's visibility timeout
      When the source is read
      Then each message's visibility is extended before it is read
      And no other reader receives a message while it is being read
      And every message is deleted once its events are written

    @unit
    Scenario: A read stops taking messages past its time or byte budget
      Given a batch of messages whose objects together pass the read's byte budget
      When the source is read
      Then the messages not yet started are handed back to the queue at once
      And they are read on the next run

  Rule: an object that cannot be read is retried, then recorded as poison

    @unit
    Scenario: An unreadable Parquet object stays on the queue until its receive limit
      Given the queue announces an object that is not a Parquet file
      When the source is read before the message reached its receive limit
      Then the message stays on the queue for another try

    @unit
Scenario: Events of the readable objects are kept when a later object becomes poison
      Given a notification with a readable object and an object that stays unreadable
      When the unreadable object reaches the receive limit
      Then the readable object's AI events are written
      And the unreadable object is recorded as poison and the message deleted

    @unit
    Scenario: An object past its receive limit is recorded as poison and deleted
      Given an object that failed as many reads as the source allows
      When the source is read again
      Then the object is recorded as poison with the reason
      And its message is deleted

    @unit
    Scenario: A corrupt object's poison reason says it is not a readable Parquet file
      Given the queue announces an object that is not a Parquet file
      When it is recorded as poison
      Then the reason says the object is not a readable Parquet file
      And it does not say the AWS request failed

    @unit
    Scenario: A Security Lake row past the OCSF event limits is counted invalid and not stored
      Given a Parquet object with a row of more than 64 KiB in UTF-8 bytes and a row with class_uid 99999999999
      When the source is read
      Then both rows are counted invalid and only the other rows are stored

    @unit
    Scenario: An object that decodes past the size or row caps is recorded as poison at once
      Given a small Parquet object that inflates past the decompressed byte cap of one object
      When the source is read
      Then decoding stops at the cap
      And the object is recorded as poison on the first read and its message deleted

    @unit
    Scenario: A message that is not a notification is recorded as poison and deleted
      Given the queue holds a message whose body is not JSON
      When the source is read
      Then it is recorded as poison and deleted

    @integration
    Scenario: A late Security Lake event reaches the SIEM export after its cursor
      Given a SIEM reader holds the cursor of the last governance event page
      When the source writes an event that happened two hours ago
      Then the next page read with that cursor returns the event

  Rule: the source runs on its own schedule and reports its health

    @unit
Scenario: Editing one Security Lake setting keeps the other stored settings
      Given a stored Security Lake source with the schedule "*/10 * * * *" and an external id
      When an admin updates only its queue URL
      Then the new queue URL is stored
      And the schedule, the external id and the limits keep their stored values

    @integration
    Scenario: An endpoint override sent as null on edit is removed
      Given a stored Security Lake source with S3 and SQS endpoint overrides
      When an admin updates it with the S3 endpoint set to null
      Then the S3 endpoint override is removed and the source reads the AWS default
      And the SQS endpoint override is kept

    @unit
    Scenario: Saving a Security Lake source gives it the pull schedule from its configuration
      Given an admin saves a Security Lake source with the schedule "*/10 * * * *"
      When the source is stored through the ingestion source flow
      Then it is stored as a security_lake source with the pull schedule "*/10 * * * *"

    @unit
    Scenario: The source is read on its pull schedule
      Given a Security Lake source with the schedule "*/5 * * * *"
      When the pull lifecycle schedules it and the scheduled pull runs
      Then the pull is scheduled on "*/5 * * * *"
      And each run reads its subscriber queue once through the Security Lake puller

    @unit
    Scenario: The source health shows the last read, lag, counts and poison objects
      Given a source that has read files and recorded one poison object
      When the admin opens the source
      Then it shows the last read time, the lag behind the oldest event written in the last run, the written and dropped counts and the poison object

    @integration
    Scenario: The written count counts each event once when a file is read again
      Given a Security Lake source that wrote the same event in two runs
      When the admin opens the source health
      Then Events written counts that event once

    @integration
    Scenario: A Security Lake source's own page counts the events it wrote
      Given a Security Lake source that wrote two events, one of them twice
      And a trace mirror row of the same source
      When the admin opens the source's own page
      Then it counts and lists the two events once each

    @unit
    Scenario: A source's own page reads its Security Lake rows with the source type bound, not spliced
      When the source's own page counts and lists the rows the source wrote
      Then each query names the Security Lake source type as a bound parameter
      And the source type is never written into the SQL text

  Rule: model callers without traces are listed

    @unit
    Scenario: A principal that calls Bedrock and sends no traces is listed as a caller without traces
      Given Security Lake events from the principal "arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/agent"
      And no trace in the organisation carries that principal
      When the admin opens the callers seen in Security Lake
      Then the principal is listed with its models, Regions and last call time

    @unit
    Scenario: The trace lookup for callers runs a few projects at a time
      Given an organisation with many live projects
      When the admin opens the callers seen in Security Lake
      Then no more than a few trace lookups run at once

    @unit
    Scenario: A Security Lake read refuses a row of the wrong shape
      Given the event store answers the callers or the written count read
      When a row's counts arrive as text and another row has the wrong shape
      Then the counts are read as numbers
      And the row of the wrong shape is refused rather than shown

  Rule: the settings in the governance sources surface

    @integration
    Scenario: The generic sources list leaves Security Lake sources to their own section
      Given an organisation with a Security Lake source and other ingestion sources
      When the admin opens the Sources pane
      Then the generic sources table lists the other sources and not the Security Lake one

    @integration
    Scenario: The settings form opens with CloudTrail management and data events checked
      Given an admin adds a Security Lake source in the governance sources settings
      When the form opens
      Then CloudTrail management, S3 data and Lambda data events are checked
      And Test connection waits until the source is saved

    @integration
    Scenario: The settings form names the malformed fields before it saves
      Given an admin enters the Region "Europe" and the role ARN "not-an-arn"
      When the admin saves the source
      Then the form names the Region and role ARN fields
      And nothing is sent

    @integration
    Scenario: The settings form shows the generated external id to copy
      Given a saved Security Lake source
      When the admin edits it
      Then the form shows the external id LangWatch generated, read-only, with a copy button
      And a new source form has no external id field

    @integration
    Scenario: Pressing Enter while the source saves sends it once
      Given an admin fills the Security Lake source form
      When the admin presses Enter twice while the first save is pending
      Then the source is sent once

    @integration
    Scenario: Test connection sends the values in the form
      Given an admin edits a saved Security Lake source and changes its queue URL
      When the admin presses Test connection
      Then the test is sent with the changed queue URL and no blank keys
      And cancelling the edit clears the test result
    @integration
    Scenario: Test connection shows the queue backlog in the settings form
      Given a saved Security Lake source whose queue holds two messages
      When the admin presses Test connection
      Then the form shows the queue reachable with two messages waiting

    @integration
    Scenario: The settings show the source health with its poison objects
      Given a source whose last run failed and recorded one poison object
      When the admin opens the Security Lake settings
      Then the panel shows the error, the lag, the counts and the poison object

    @integration
    Scenario: The health card says a read wrote no new events rather than showing no lag
      Given a source whose last read wrote no events
      When the admin opens the Security Lake settings
      Then the lag says there were no new events
      And before the first read it says the source was not read yet

    @integration
    Scenario: An organisation without Enterprise is not offered a Security Lake source to add
      Given an organisation without the Enterprise plan
      When an admin opens the Security Lake settings
      Then no Add Security Lake source or Edit is offered
      And the section says the Enterprise plan unlocks it

    @integration
    Scenario: A Security Lake source's own page offers no secret to rotate
      Given a Security Lake source, which has no ingest secret
      When an admin opens the source's own page
      Then Rotate secret is not offered
      And Edit opens the Security Lake settings instead of the generic edit drawer

    @integration
    Scenario: With no Security Lake source the callers list asks to connect one
      Given an organisation with no Security Lake source
      When the admin opens the Security Lake settings
      Then the callers list says "Connect a Security Lake source to see model callers"
      And no callers are read

    @integration
    Scenario: The settings list each caller without traces with its models and Regions
      Given a principal that calls models in Security Lake and sends no traces
      When the admin opens the Security Lake settings
      Then the callers list shows the principal with its models, Regions and call count
